        <span>⚡</span> Quick Answer: The Panamanian Territorial Advantage
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-200 text-base leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Under <strong>Article 694 of the Panamanian Tax Code</strong>, income generated from foreign software sales, remote consulting, and international investments carries a statutory <strong>0% tax rate</strong>. For remote founders, the <strong>Friendly Nations Visa</strong> offers a $200,000 real estate or employment path with a 2-year provisional card, while the <strong>Qualified Investor Visa</strong> grants immediate permanent residency in 30 days via a $300,000 property investment.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-12">
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-200">
        In the landscape of international tax engineering, very few sovereign states maintain a pure, uncorrupted territorial tax doctrine. While Caribbean island nations have introduced corporate taxes under OECD Pillar Two pressure, and European hubs like Portugal have phased out legacy non-habitual regimes, the Republic of Panama stands as one of the world's most enduring and legally grounded territorial jurisdictions.
      </p>
      <p>
        For solo SaaS founders, digital media operators, and remote engineers earning six-to-seven-figure revenue streams, Panama presents an extraordinary proposition: absolute statutory exemption from income tax, dividend tax, and capital gains on all offshore commercial activities, codified not through temporary administrative decrees, but in the constitutional and fiscal bedrock of <strong>Article 694 of the Código Fiscal</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        However, selecting the appropriate immigration vehicle to anchor this fiscal sanctuary has become significantly more complex following legislative updates including <strong>Executive Decree 197 of 2021</strong> (which reformed the storied Friendly Nations Visa) and <strong>Executive Decree 722 of 2020</strong> (the Qualified Investor Visa, or <em>Inversionista Calificado</em>).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Territorial Tax Doctrine -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      1. The Territorial Tax Doctrine: Deciphering Article 694 of the Código Fiscal
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        The statutory core of Panama’s fiscal sovereignty is established in Article 694, Paragraph 1 of the Tax Code:
      </p>
      <blockquote class="border-l-4 border-emerald-500 bg-slate-900/60 p-4 rounded-r-xl italic text-slate-200 text-sm">
        "Es objeto del Impuesto sobre la Renta la renta gravable que se produzca, de cualquier fuente, dentro del territorio de la República de Panamá sea cual fuere el lugar donde se perciba."
        <span class="block not-italic text-xs text-emerald-400 font-mono mt-2">
          (Taxable income for Income Tax purposes is that produced, from any source, within the territory of the Republic of Panama, regardless of the place where it is received.)
        </span>
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        Crucially, Paragraph 2 establishes the non-taxation of extraterritorial activities, defining operations that explicitly do <strong>not</strong> produce taxable income in Panama:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Invoicing and Direction of Offshore Sales:</strong> Directing commercial transactions from an office in Panama, where the merchandise or underlying services never physically enter Panamanian territory.</li>
        <li><strong>Offshore Financial Transactions:</strong> Distributing dividends or receiving yields from funds, securities, and financial assets held abroad, even if bank wires transit through Panamanian banks.</li>
        <li><strong>International Software and SaaS Deliverables:</strong> Providing software-as-a-service, algorithmic code, API access, or digital subscriptions hosted on cloud infrastructure (AWS, Cloudflare, Google Cloud) to customers situated outside the national perimeter.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Unlike the United States (citizenship-based taxation) or Western Europe (worldwide taxation based on residency), a tax resident of Panama can earn $1,000,000 USD per year from a Delaware C-Corp or Stripe account and incur a legally validated tax liability of <strong>$0.00</strong> to the Dirección General de Ingresos (DGI).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Empirical Table 1: Territorial vs Worldwide Comparison -->
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Empirical Tax Exposure by Revenue Stream: Panama vs Traditional Jurisdictions</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-1 shadow-2xl mb-8">
