    <!-- H2: Friendly Nations Visa vs Qualified Investor -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      2. Visa Showdown: Friendly Nations (Decree 197) vs Qualified Investor (Decree 722)
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Until August 2021, the Friendly Nations Visa (<em>Visa de Países Amigos</em>) was the undisputed premier residency route for Western nationals. Applicants could establish a simple Panamanian corporation, deposit $5,000 in a local bank, and receive immediate permanent residency.
      </p>
      <p>
        The enactment of <strong>Executive Decree 197 of 2021</strong> dismantled the low-cost corporate formation loophole. Today, remote founders must choose between two distinct legal frameworks:
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
        <!-- Friendly Nations Card -->
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-6 relative">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-cyan-400 font-bold mb-2">Decree 197 of 2021</div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Friendly Nations Visa (PFN)</h3>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li><strong>Eligible Nationalities:</strong> 50 recognized friendly nations (US, UK, Canada, Australia, EU states, etc.).</li>
            <li><strong>Investment Threshold:</strong> $200,000 USD in Panamanian real estate OR a $200,000 fixed-term deposit in a licensed Panamanian bank (minimum 3-year term).</li>
            <li><strong>Alternative Route (Labor):</strong> Formal employment contract with a Panamanian corporation + Ministry of Labor (MITRADEL) work permit.</li>
            <li><strong>Residency Staging:</strong> 2-year provisional card first; permanent residency application only upon month 24.</li>
            <li><strong>Processing Timeline:</strong> 4 to 6 months per phase.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="text-[11px] text-slate-400 bg-slate-950 p-2.5 rounded border border-slate-800">
            <em>Best for:</em> Founders willing to buy property or commit a $200k CD who don't mind a 2-year transition period before permanent status.
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Qualified Investor Card -->
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-emerald-500/40 rounded-xl p-6 relative">
          <div class="text-xs font-mono uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400 font-bold mb-2">Executive Decree 722 of 2020</div>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Qualified Investor Visa (Red Carpet)</h3>
          <ul class="text-xs text-slate-300 space-y-2 mb-4">
            <li><strong>Eligible Nationalities:</strong> Universal (open to all global citizens without friendly-nation restrictions).</li>
            <li><strong>Investment Options:</strong>
              <br/>• Real Estate: $300,000 USD unencumbered (increasing to $500,000).
              <br/>• Panamanian Stock Market (BVP): $500,000 in approved securities.
              <br/>• Fixed Deposit: $750,000 in a local bank (minimum 5-year lockup).
            </li>
            <li><strong>Residency Staging:</strong> Direct Permanent Residency granted immediately.</li>
            <li><strong>Processing Timeline:</strong> Statutory fast-track resolution within <strong>30 calendar days</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Remote Application:</strong> Can be filed entirely via Panamanian legal power of attorney before traveling.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="text-[11px] text-emerald-300/80 bg-emerald-950/30 p-2.5 rounded border border-emerald-500/30">
            <em>Best for:</em> High-net-worth founders, crypto investors, and rapid relocators demanding immediate permanent status and swift processing.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Empirical Table 2: Detailed Architectural Comparison -->
    <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4">Empirical Feature Comparison: PFN vs Qualified Investor vs Remote Worker Visa</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-1 shadow-2xl mb-8">
